WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.085
Detection engineering requires measurement because teams need evidence that their detections are useful.

00:00:05.085 --> 00:00:08.153
Metrics help teams understand quality, coverage, operational performance,

00:00:08.153 --> 00:00:10.837
and where improvement work should be prioritized.

00:00:10.837 --> 00:00:15.056
Useful measures may include false positive patterns, analyst feedback, investigation outcomes,

00:00:15.056 --> 00:00:19.274
coverage analysis, alert volume trends, data quality issues, time spent tuning,

00:00:19.274 --> 00:00:20.425
and lifecycle health.

00:00:20.425 --> 00:00:23.876
Metrics should support better decisions, not create vanity dashboards.

00:00:23.876 --> 00:00:28.094
A large number of detections does not automatically mean strong visibility.

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:32.696
A low alert count does not automatically mean the environment is quiet.

00:00:32.696 --> 00:00:36.914
Analyst feedback is one of the most valuable sources of improvement.

00:00:36.914 --> 00:00:40.749
If responders consistently need missing context, if alerts are unclear,

00:00:40.749 --> 00:00:44.584
or if a detection no longer maps to current systems,

00:00:44.584 --> 00:00:46.501
the detection should be reviewed.

00:00:46.501 --> 00:00:48.419
Measurement creates a feedback loop.

00:00:48.419 --> 00:00:52.254
Teams observe how detections perform, make changes, validate those changes,

00:00:52.254 --> 00:00:54.171
and document what they learned.

00:00:54.171 --> 00:00:58.006
Over time, that loop improves confidence, maintainability, and operational effectiveness.

00:00:58.006 --> 00:01:00.690
Coverage analysis is another important measurement area.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.991
Teams compare important behaviors, assets, identities,

00:01:02.991 --> 00:01:07.593
and platforms against current detections to understand where visibility is strong and

00:01:07.593 --> 00:01:09.894
where additional work may be needed.

00:01:09.894 --> 00:01:12.195
Metrics should be interpreted with judgment.

00:01:12.195 --> 00:01:16.796
A noisy detection may still be valuable if it reveals real risk,

00:01:16.796 --> 00:01:21.398
while a quiet detection may need testing to confirm that the underlying

00:01:21.398 --> 00:01:23.316
telemetry and logic still function.

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:26.383
Improvement work should be prioritized like engineering work.

00:01:26.383 --> 00:01:30.602
Teams can weigh operational pain, security value, platform effort, dependency risk,

00:01:30.602 --> 00:01:34.820
and expected benefit before deciding what to tune or build next.
